<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Douglas MacArthur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Douglas MacArthur was an American general who served as military adviser to the Commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from 1935 and commanded Allied forces in the South-West Pacific during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a> he directed the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1945 to 1951, overseeing disarmament and a new democratic constitution. He was dismissed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur.jpg" alt="Douglas MacArthur" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer not credited. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacArthur_Manila_(cropped2)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Douglas MacArthur was born on 26 January 1880 at Little Rock Barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, the youngest of three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-macarthur-jr">Arthur MacArthur Jr.</a>, an army captain who received the Medal of Honor for the Battle of Missionary Ridge, and Mary Pinkney Hardy MacArthur. He graduated first in his class from the United States Military Academy in 1903 and joined the 3rd Engineer Battalion, which sailed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> that October. In 1914 he conducted a reconnaissance during the American occupation of Veracruz for which he was nominated for the Medal of Honor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he joined the 42nd Division, became a brigadier general on 26 June 1918 at 38,. He was Superintendent of West Point from 1919, became the army's youngest major general at 45 in 1925, and was sworn in as Chief of Staff on 21 November 1930. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> gained semi-independent status in 1935, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel Quezon</a> asked him to supervise the creation of a Philippine Army, and he became a field marshal of it.</p><p>S. Army Forces in the Far East. Much of his air force was destroyed in December 1941, and he escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in March 1942, where on 18 April he was made Supreme Commander of the Southwest Pacific Area. In October 1944 he led the campaign that liberated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-commander-for-the-allied-powers">Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> he worked through the Japanese government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Emperor Hirohito</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> shielded the emperor from war responsibility. His headquarters drafted a constitution in 1946, which took effect on 3 May 1947. It made the emperor a figure acting on ministerial advice, renounced war in Article 9 and enfranchised women. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> he led the United Nations Command from 1950, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> entered the war he was removed by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> in April 1951. On 19 April he gave a farewell address to Congress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>MacArthur died at Walter Reed Army Medical Center on 5 April 1964, aged 84. An estimated 150,000 people filed past his body as it lay in state at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>. His legacy is contested. Walter R. Borneman wrote that a public stunned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> needed a hero and embraced him. He is one of only five people to hold the rank of General of the Army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Douglas MacArthur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Douglas MacArthur</b> is quoted as saying that he always kept a copy on his desk, and Colin Powell as saying that it continues to influence both soldiers and politicians.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-art-of-war">The Art of War · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>On Hoover's orders, troops under <b>Douglas MacArthur</b> used tear gas and bayonets to clear camps of First World War veterans demanding early payment of bonuses.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>American troops landed on Leyte, and General <b>Douglas MacArthur</b> waded ashore to fulfil his 1942 promise to return to the Philippines.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-20">20 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Douglas MacArthur</b> built up a Philippine army.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-the-philippines">Commonwealth of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied, overwhelmingly American, forces occupied the home islands under the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, General <b>Douglas MacArthur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/occupied-japan">Occupied Japan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Little Rock, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.74,-92.29&amp;year=1934" aria-label="Little Rock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2208 906 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Douglas MacArthur"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2658 1176h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2658 1176h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.74,-92.29&amp;year=1934">What was here in 1934? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
